__The main difference of `Assert` and `Debug.Assert` is that `Assert` works with `#if DEBUG` symbol:__  
When you build a release version of your application, `Assert` code will be skipped because `Debug` symbol is not defined. But `Debug.Assert` code wouldn't be skipped, even in release builds.

> For more in depth knowledge look at [.Net debug assertions](https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/system.diagnostics.debug.assert?view=net-5.0)

Remember to always include such assertion inside try-catch blocks where the control may be moving around.

### What is the use of Assertions & Debugging in Unit Test?

Assertions provide a way to make checks that your code is 'correct' during the execution of the program. They are typically used in a unit test environment to enforce certain criteria about your code under certain conditions. If the assertion condition fails, it will typically halt the program and provide a message indicating what failed, usually for debugging purposes.

Unit tests should be based on concrete, expected results. Assertions allow the programmer to prove (assert) whether the implementation meets the requirements. For example, if you have a method that should return a non-null value then you could assert that that method does indeed return such value.

Using Debuggers and Assertions in your programs will make debugging a less time-consuming process. Breakpoints, watches and the value of variables can all be examined which are not always possible otherwise.

Assertions provide a mechanism to catch errors before the program reaches a customer or final release. In essence, the program will fail if conditions are not met which forces developers to understand and properly design the program. Hence, Assertions are an important and beneficial part of the software development process.

### Importance of a Good Unit Test

Software testing is important as it assures that the delivered software is working as expected and meets the business requirements. It also helps in ensuring that changes or modifications in the software product are not causing any unforeseen issues.

An effective unit test improves the quality of the software, and minimally interrupts the operations of the system under test. It also verifies the system in a defined state at the start of each test - known as **arrangement**. This is a useful approach to TDD (Test-Driven Development), in which you do write the tests before the code.

Good unit tests:

1. Reduce the time and cost of debugging.
2. Encourage better team communication
3. Highlight potential flaws before full system testing.
4. Provide documentation about what the code does.
5. Ensure the software adheres to acceptance criteria.
6. Make it easier to isolate and fix defects.

### How to write a good unit test?

1. **Isolated**: It should not be dependent on any external factors.
2. **Repeatable**: The test case should produce the same result on different run-throughs or the same run-through.
3. **Self-checking**: The test case should determine whether it passed (or failed) by itself.
4. **Timely**: It should be written as soon as the code is written.
5. **Suitable**: It should be suitable for the level of code that is being tested.
6. **Manageable**: It should be reasonably straightforward and focused.

### Examples of Unit Test in C#

Firstly, you need to import the correct unit testing framework. The MS Test is a popular one. Let's take a simple example of a method--------------------	Debug.Assert(templatesFileExist);

	if (templatesFileExist)
	{
		var xelement =  XElement.Load(fileName);
		// we know that we have a collection of 'ItemTemplate' elements, 
		// there can be more validation tests performed in here using xml linq methods.
		var itemTemplates = from template in xelement.Elements("ItemTemplate")
							select new
							{
								Name = (string)template.Element("Name"),
								Value = (string)template.Element("Value"),
								IsHidden = (string)template.Element("IsHidden"),
								HelpText = (string)template.Element("HelpText")
							};
		if (itemTemplates != null)
		{
			InitializeTemplates(itemTemplates);
		}
	}
	// return result, whether file exists or not.
}


// example of the initialization
private void InitializeTemplates(IEnumerable<dynamic> itemTemplates)
{
	foreach (var template in itemTemplates)
	{
		Type templateType = typeof(DefaultItemTemplate);
		// perform any necessary switch over the Name property to determine 
		// what real type of template will be initialized
		// then create an instance of this type using reflection
		BaseTemplate newTemplate = (BaseTemplate)Activator.CreateInstance(templateType);
		newTemplate.Value = template.Value;
		newTemplate.Name = template.Name;
		newTemplate.IsHidden = bool.Parse(template.IsHidden);
		newTemplate.HelpText = template.HelpText;
		templates.Add(newTemplate);
	}
}


// example of class definition, not shown is inheritance from the class BaseTemplate
public class DefaultItemTemplate : BaseTemplate
{
	.... 
	// some implementation for the DefaultItemTemplate class.
}


// example of generic base class.
public class BaseTemplate
{
	public string Name { get; set; }
	public string Value { get; set; }
	public bool IsHidden { get; set; }
	public string HelpText { get; set; }
	.... 
}
```

Here we are loading an XML file with templates. We are using `File.Exists` to check if the template file exists. 

We then use a `LINQ to XML` query on the root (parent) element of the xml file in order to select (`select`) only the `ItemTemplate` children of the root. 

These `ItemTemplate` children are then transformed into anonymous types that we store within the `itemTemplates` variable.

These anonymous types then passed to the `InitializeTemplates` method, where we create instances of templates with different types depending on the type specified in Name property.

This is done using reflection (`Activator.CreateInstance`). 

This example assumes that every child of `ItemTemplate` has `Name`, `Value`, `IsHidden` and `HelpText` elements. Also we can have different types of templates distinguished by `Name` property which determines what type of template will be initialized. 

This design pattern uses reflection and lambda expressions extensively and allows developer to create a new template just by implementing new class inherited from `BaseTemplate`.

**NOTE** Make sure that your xml schema matches with what you will be parsing. If it doesn't match, things like `template.Element("Name")` might throw `NullReferenceException` if the element does not exist. Make sure to handle those scenarios approprietely.
 Also consider catching actual exception rather than relying on Debug.Assert(true) for file existing since in release mode it might block the execution.
```

